# P3: the t=4 -> t=8 inversion on perf/p2-kernels — what it was, and the fix

date: 2026-08-07
host: Apple M4 Pro (8 P-core + 4 E-core = 12 logical), macOS 26.5.2, aarch64
rustc: stable, release (lto="fat", codegen-units=1), DEFAULT features
       (bitdepth_8 + bitdepth_16, CHECKED DisjointMut). No -C target-cpu=native.
branch: perf/p3-t8-inversion, off perf/p2-kernels @ 0624dfd.
vectors: ~/tmp/rav1d-perf/vec/v4k_8tile{,_10b}.avif — 3840x2160, 4 tile cols x
         2 tile rows. Same files as the P1/P2 campaigns.
follows: benchmarks/p2_kernels_2026-08-07.meta (which reported the inversion),
         benchmarks/p1_barrier_2026-08-07.meta (which removed the barrier that
         had been hiding it).

## nice level, and a box that was not idle

NO `nice` ON ANY TIMED RUN. Darwin maps a positive nice value to background QoS
and distorts wall clock by ~40x; `nice -n 19` is for BUILDS only.

Two classes of run below, and they are labelled:

* **IDLE-BOX.** `scripts/perf/p3_sweep.sh` with its default guard: the cell is
  discarded and re-run while any non-agent process is over 25% CPU. Every
  number in "The diagnosis" and "What the fix is made of" is one of these.
* **CONTENDED-BOX.** The final confirmation campaign ran while two sibling
  agents were running their own `bench_*` sweeps on this machine continuously
  (~2 of 12 cores, on and off, for the whole session), and a strict guard
  discarded the same cell 4 times in a row without ever committing. That
  campaign used `ALLOW=` to treat those two binaries as non-foreign. Arms are
  still interleaved back to back with a rotating order inside each cell, which
  is what lets an A/B survive a roughly stationary background load — it does
  NOT make the absolute ms/frame comparable to an idle-box run, and the two
  are not mixed in any table.

## What was wrong

perf/p2-kernels, v4k_8tile 8bpc, ms/frame, IDLE-BOX, median of 9
(`benchmarks/p3_baseline_2026-08-07.tsv`):

  t        1       2       4       8      12      16
  head 411.9   259.6   176.8   189.1   202.3   208.2

t=4 was the best configuration and every thread past it made things worse.
10bpc did not invert (t=4 224.8 -> t=8 212.1).

## The diagnosis — three instruments, one answer

### 1. Occupancy is fine; the WORK inflates

`probe-tasktime` (the P1 task profile, `src/probe_tasktime.rs`), per frame:

  t     tile_recon  deblock_cols  deblock_rows   cdef   total CPU   wall   mean_active
  4          713.1         106.4          45.2   67.7       932.3  236.1     3.95 / 4
  8         1081.1         224.5          70.0  135.6      1511.2  200.0     7.55 / 8
  16        1244.8         661.4          55.5  122.6      2084.3  231.4     8.42 / 16

The task COUNTS are identical at every thread count — 136 tile-recon plus 34
each of deblock-cols / deblock-rows / cdef — and `defer deblock_barrier` is 0,
so the P1 barrier really is gone and the pool really is fed. What changes is
the cost of the same work: **+62% CPU from t=4 to t=8, +124% to t=16.** The
filter chain is no longer serial either (`filtconc` sees up to 8 concurrent
filter tasks at t=8), so this is not an Amdahl term.

`superres` and `loop_restore` are 0.000 ms on this vector, as before.

### 2. `sample` says the inflation is the borrow tracker

v4k_8tile 8bpc t=8, 6 s, self-time, `scripts/perf/sample_selftime.py`.
`__psynch_cvwait` (15.91% raw) is idle parking and is excluded from the
denominator below; percentages are of the 30,978 non-idle leaf samples:

  TinyLock::lock_slow                       23.2%
  BorrowTracker::add::<false> + ::<true>    24.3%
  CompInterType guard drop glue             10.2%
  decode_coefs                              16.8%

At t=1 the same two `add` arms are 7.8% and `lock_slow` does not appear at all.
So it is not only the spin: **the uncontended fast path itself gets 3x more
expensive**, which is the signature of a cache line being written by eight
cores rather than of a lock being queued on.

### 3. Removing the tracker removes the inversion

A `probe-untracked` build (the tracker compiled out — measurement only, never
shippable) against head, IDLE-BOX, median of 9
(`benchmarks/p3_shard_isolate_2026-08-07.tsv`):

  v4k_8tile 8bpc      t=1     t=2     t=4     t=8    t=16
  head (32 shards)  412.5   261.1   175.3   190.5   209.9
  untracked         336.5   184.3    95.5    57.7    65.2
  64 shards         447.1   257.9   163.7   162.0   172.3
  128 shards        522.5   238.5   139.1   123.5   135.6

  v4k_8tile 10bpc                     t=4     t=8
  head                              224.9   213.6
  untracked                         144.8    81.4
  128 shards                        191.0   153.9

Without the tracker there is no inversion at all (5.83x from t=1 to t=8) and
the wall is 57.7 ms against dav1d's 34.6. **The inversion is the tracker, and
at t=8 the tracker is ~70% of our wall.** More shards fixes it; it costs at
t=1.

**This supersedes the leading hypothesis in the P3 brief.** The brief expected
an un-backed-off `TinyLock` to be most of the win. It is a symptom, not the
cause: backoff re-tested on top of the shard fix is again inside noise
(see "Idle-box confirmation"), exactly as the P1 campaign found.

### 4. The t=1 cost of more shards is NOT cache footprint

The obvious reading of the 522.5 above is that a 128-shard table (16 KiB per
instance, 12 hot picture planes) no longer fits L1. It is not that. An arm with
`N_SHARDS = 128` and only 32 of them reachable — same table, same allocation,
one quarter of the lines ever touched — measures the same as using all 128
(IDLE-BOX, median of 9, `benchmarks/p3_shard_mask_2026-08-07.tsv`):

  arm                                     t=1     t=4     t=8
  head            (32 shards, 32 used)  413.7   176.2   189.0
  128 shards, 128 used                  523.7   138.5   124.7
  128 shards,  32 used                  528.0   178.3   193.0

The 32-used arm gets the t=1 penalty AND none of the t=8 win. So the penalty is
proportional to the shard count the code *walks*, not to the table it allocates
— and the only thing that walks every shard is the wide path, `add_wide` /
`remove_wide`, which locks and scans all `N_SHARDS`.

Wide borrows are not rare at t=1, and the reason is structural: with tile
threading OFF, `WithOffset::block_mut` takes the strided guard over
`(h - 1) * stride + w`, which for a 16x16 block on a 3840-wide plane is 15
blocks — past `MAX_SHARDS_PER_BORROW = 4`, hence wide. Under tile threading the
same call takes the compact per-row path, whose borrows are single-block. That
is why the shard count is expensive exactly where it buys nothing.
`sample` on the 128-shard build at t=1 confirms it: `add_wide` 12.55% +
`remove_wide` 10.14% = 22.7% of self time, against 7.16% for the narrow `add`.

## The fix

Two changes, commit 14873a6.

**(a) The wide path holds `0..=mask`, not the whole array.** `shard_of` ends in
`& self.mask`, so every record an instance stores and every shard lock a narrow
registrant of that instance takes are inside that prefix; the rest of the array
is unreachable for it. Holding the prefix is exactly the exclusion `add_wide`
needs. For a sub-`SHARD_MIN_LEN` instance that is 1 lock instead of 128.

**(b) `set_parallelism(n_tc)`**, called from `rav1d_open` beside
`set_tile_threading` and monotone for the same reason that one had to become
monotone (P2 §"Soundness finding"): 32 active shards for a serial decode, the
full 128 for a threaded one. Read once per `BorrowTracker::new` and immutable
for that tracker's life.

(a) alone, at the old 32 shards, is a null — IDLE-BOX, median of 9,
`benchmarks/p3_widepath_2026-08-07.tsv`:

  arm       8bpc t=1   t=4     t=8    10bpc t=8
  head         412.7  176.0   189.3      213.4
  +wide-narrow 418.3  175.5   188.0      213.2

It is worth landing anyway because it is what makes (b) affordable: with (a) in
place, a 128-shard build restricted to 32 active shards costs 431.0 ms at t=1
instead of 531.3 — 100 of the 118 ms penalty recovered. The residual 17 ms
(+4.2% at t=1) is the bigger array itself and is NOT recovered; see "What is
still open".

## Confirmation campaign

CONTENDED-BOX (see "nice level"), 4 arms interleaved back to back with the
order rotating each round, 3 rounds x 3 reps x 3 iters = MEDIAN OF 9 per
cell/arm, 288 rows. Raw: `benchmarks/p3_adapt_2026-08-07.tsv`.

  arms:  head  = perf/p2-kernels @ 0624dfd, unmodified (32 shards)
         adapt = 14873a6, the shipped change (128 shards, adaptive mask)
         a64   = 14873a6 + `--features shards-64`
         a32   = 14873a6 + `--features shards-32` — the control that isolates
                 the shard-count bump from everything else in the commit

  v4k_8tile 8bpc     t=1     t=2     t=4     t=8    t=16
  head             413.8   262.8   175.9   190.0   209.9
  adapt            434.1   240.5   140.3   125.0   135.9
  a64              421.8   259.4   163.5   160.0   171.7
  a32              418.8   262.4   175.6   188.6   210.9
  adapt vs head    +4.9%   -8.5%  -20.2%  -34.2%  -35.3%

  v4k_8tile 10bpc    t=1                     t=4     t=8
  head             601.5                   224.8   229.1*
  adapt            621.0                   191.9   171.4*
  a64              610.2                   208.7   187.0
  a32              609.0                   225.4   214.3

  * the 10bpc t=8 cell is the one that visibly caught background load in the
    third round — head re-measures at 212.1 (baseline) / 213.6 (isolate) /
    212.9 (this campaign's first two rounds) on a quiet box, and adapt at
    157.1. The direction is not in doubt but treat that single cell's
    absolute numbers as the least reliable in the table.

Reading it:

* **The inversion is gone.** 8bpc t=4 140.3 -> t=8 125.0 -> t=16 135.9: t=8 is
  now the best configuration, as it should be, and t=16 (12 logical cores, so
  oversubscribed) degrades gracefully instead of continuing to climb.
* **Best shippable configuration: 175.9 ms (head at t=4) -> 125.0 ms (adapt at
  t=8) = 1.41x.** Against head's best at ANY thread count, the same number.
* **a32 reproduces head at every cell** (418.8/262.4/175.6/188.6/210.9 against
  413.8/262.8/175.9/190.0/209.9, and it still inverts). So the win is the shard
  count and nothing else in the commit; the wide-path narrowing and the latch
  cost ~1% at t=1 and are otherwise inert.
* 64 shards is the intermediate it looks like: half the win, half the t=1 cost,
  and it still does not invert. Kept available as `--features shards-64`.
* **Bit identity: 288/288 invocations across 4 arms x 8 cells x 5 thread counts
  agree** — one md5 per vector (`a00c11f4...` 8bpc, `840d4364...` 10bpc), which
  are the same two hashes the P1 and P2 campaigns recorded.

## Idle-box confirmation, and the `TinyLock` backoff arm

The campaign above was run under sibling load, so the headline cells were
re-run with the STRICT guard — 7 cells discarded and re-run before committing —
on the same three-arm interleave. IDLE-BOX, median of 9, 135 rows. Raw:
`benchmarks/p3_confirm_idle_2026-08-07.tsv`.

  ms/frame            8bpc t=1     t=4     t=8    t=16   10bpc t=8
  head                   413.3   175.8   187.0   208.8       213.7
  adapt                  430.5   139.7   125.3   135.6       161.7
  adapt + backoff        428.8   139.0   124.9   132.4       162.2
  adapt vs head          +4.2%  -20.5%  -33.0%  -35.1%      -24.3%

This reproduces the contended campaign to within 0.5% on every `head` and
`adapt` cell except 10bpc t=8, so the two are consistent and the contended
table can be read at face value.

**Best shippable configuration: 175.8 ms (head, t=4) -> 125.3 ms (adapt, t=8),
1.40x, with t=8 restored to being the best thread count.**

**MEASURED NEGATIVE — `TinyLock` spin backoff (yield to the scheduler after 64
spins) is inside noise, again.** This was the P3 brief's leading hypothesis and
its first candidate fix. On top of the shard change: -0.4% at t=1, -0.5% at
t=4, -0.3% at t=8, +0.3% at 10bpc t=8. The only cell outside noise is t=16
(-2.4%), which is oversubscribed on a 12-logical-core box and is one cell — not
grounds for a spin-policy change. The P1 campaign reached the same verdict on
the 32-shard base (`benchmarks/p1_barrier_lock_ab_2026-08-07.tsv`: t=8 276.8 ->
274.7). Kept as `--features probe-lock-backoff` so the next person can re-test
without rebuilding the argument; NOT enabled.

The reason it keeps measuring null is visible in the diagnosis above: at t=8 on
the OLD shard count, `add`'s uncontended fast path was itself 3x its
single-thread cost. That is cache-line ownership traffic, which a waiter's
politeness does not touch. Spreading the borrows over 4x the lines does.

## Against dav1d

Same instrument on both sides, measured here rather than quoted: each stream
run at 2 and 20 frames and `total = alpha + beta*frames` fitted so process
startup drops out. dav1d 1.5.4 (homebrew) driven with `--framedelay 1` so it
runs the tile-threading-only model we do. IDLE-BOX guard, arms rotated. n is
the completed round count per cell (2 or 3 — the campaign was cut short by
sibling load; the raw file is `benchmarks/p3_dav1d_2026-08-07.tsv`).

  ms/frame                 head    adapt    dav1d   head/dav1d   adapt/dav1d
  v4k_8tile      t=1     412.7    434.1    246.7        1.67x         1.76x
  v4k_8tile      t=8     187.7    123.6     35.9        5.23x         3.44x
  v4k_8tile_10b  t=1     601.8    621.1    251.6        2.39x         2.47x
  v4k_8tile_10b  t=8     212.4    158.6     38.2        5.65x         4.18x

  t1 -> t8 scaling      head 2.20x   adapt 3.51x   dav1d 6.87x

**The 8-thread gap goes 5.23x -> 3.44x at 8bpc and 5.65x -> 4.18x at 10bpc.**
Single-thread moves the wrong way by the 4-5% the bigger shard array costs.

This instrument reproduces the interleaved sweep to within 0.5% on all four of
our cells (412.7 vs 413.3, 187.7 vs 187.0, 434.1 vs 430.5, 123.6 vs 125.3) and
re-measures dav1d to within 0.04% of the 246.7 ms and 3.8% of the 34.6 ms on
record from the P2 campaign — so this and the P1/P2 campaigns are directly
comparable.

## What is still open / not done

* **The +4.2% single-thread residual is real and unexplained beyond "the array
  is 4x bigger".** 431.0 vs 413.7 ms at t=1, IDLE-BOX. Sizing the shard array
  itself at runtime (`Box<[Shard]>`) would remove it, but the array is a
  fixed-size field precisely so a masked index needs no fat-pointer load and no
  bounds check on the hottest path in the decoder, and that trade was not
  measured here. Not attempted.
* **The tracker is still ~70% of the t=8 wall.** 123.5 ms with the fix against
  57.7 ms with the tracker compiled out. Shard count is a mitigation, not a
  cure; the cure is fewer borrows. The two named volume targets from the
  profile are `CompInterType`'s guard drop glue (10.2% of non-idle at t=8) and
  `block_mut`'s compact path, which under tile threading takes h per-row
  IMMUTABLE guards to read plus h per-row MUTABLE guards to write back —
  double the tracker traffic of the scalar path it replaced, for the same
  pixels. Neither was attempted.
* **The itx8 t=8 regression named in P2 is explained but not fixed.** P2
  measured `itx8` slower than its base at t=8 (211.6 vs 200.2) while much
  faster at t=1. The NEON arm routes through `block_mut`, i.e. 16 per-row
  guards plus a copy in and a copy out; the scalar `inv_txfm_add` takes 8
  per-row guards and no copy. Holding the h per-row MUTABLE guards across the
  kernel instead of taking immutable ones and then mutable ones would halve
  that, and is sound (a write guard is strictly stronger than the read guard it
  replaces), but it was not implemented or measured.
* **10bpc.** The fix is bit-depth-agnostic and 10bpc improves too, but 10bpc
  never inverted, so nothing here is validated as "fixing" a 10bpc problem.

## Measured negatives

* **`TinyLock` backoff — the brief's leading hypothesis — is again inside
  noise.** Numbers under "Idle-box confirmation".
* **A 128-shard array with a 32-shard active set** gets the single-thread cost
  and none of the multi-thread win (528.0 / 178.3 / 193.0). Included because it
  is the experiment that falsified the cache-footprint explanation.
* **The wide-path narrowing on its own** is a null at 32 shards (above).

## Correctness

* **The wide path's exclusion is tested by a race, not asserted.**
  `crates/rav1d-disjoint-mut/tests/wide_exclusion.rs` runs one thread taking
  wide mutable borrows of the whole buffer against six taking narrow mutable
  borrows scattered across it, and fails if a narrow borrow is ever granted
  while the wide holder provably still holds. A missed overlap here is SILENT —
  both borrows simply succeed — so the test looks for the witness, not for a
  panic. 20/20 clean on the shipped code.
* **Liveness of that gate, both directions.** Deliberately shortening the
  prefix fails it: `..1` produces 5 witnesses, `..half` produces 2. So it
  discriminates at the granularity the change actually operates on, not just
  against a total removal.
* **HONEST NEGATIVE: the single-threaded unit tests do NOT catch either
  break.** `wide_and_narrow_still_see_each_other_on_a_one_shard_instance` and
  `..._at_full_width` both PASS with the prefix broken to `..1`, because a
  narrow registrant finds a wide record through the `state` word and the side
  list regardless of which shards the wide registrant locked — the locking is a
  race property. They are kept because they pin the side-list half at both mask
  widths, but they are not evidence about the narrowing. That is why the race
  gate is a separate file.
* **Full corpus, bit identity.** `cargo test --release --no-fail-fast --
  --test-threads=1` on this branch produces **4,945 `MISMATCH [tier]: <id>
  expected=.. actual=..` lines and 2,845 distinct (tier, id, actual) triples** —
  the exact counts `benchmarks/p2_kernels_2026-08-07.meta` recorded for
  0624dfd's lineage on both sides of its own A/B. The failing set is the
  documented 34.
* **`mt_stress_4k` passes at threads 1/2/4/8/16.** It appeared as a 35th
  failure on the first suite run for an environment reason, not a code one:
  `test-vectors/` is gitignored and a fresh worktree has no
  `test-vectors/bench/photo_4k.avif`. Symlinking it from the primary checkout
  and re-running is green. Worth knowing before reading a fresh worktree's
  test output as a regression.
* **Concurrent-process overlap stress on the shipped build:** 6 concurrent
  `tile_threading_overlap --ignored` processes x 4 rounds, **0/24 FAILED**
  (`scripts/perf/p2_concurrent_overlap.sh`, the P2 instrument; the same script
  showed 8-9/24 on the pre-latch build). `tile_threading_parity` 3/3 and
  `tile_threading_overlap` 3/3.
* All 20 `tracker_shard` unit tests pass, including
  `cross_shard_overlaps_are_all_caught`, `wide_borrow_is_visible_to_narrow_registrants`
  and `narrow_borrow_is_visible_to_wide_registrant`; all 5
  `tests/shard_liveness.rs` tests pass, including `concurrent_overlaps_are_caught`.
* **Cross-vector bit identity, adapt vs head:** all six local vectors
  (v4k_8tile, v4k_8tile_10b, v4k_1tile, v4k_1tile_10b, v1024, v1024_10b,
  v256) decode to the same md5 on both arms at t=1 and t=8, and v4k_8tile /
  v4k_8tile_10b are identical at t=1/2/4/8/16 as well.
* **The race gate under process pressure:** 6 concurrent `wide_exclusion`
  processes x 4 rounds, 0/24 FAILED.
* `set_parallelism` is monotone, pinned by `set_parallelism_is_monotone`.
* clippy `--release --all-targets` reports 82 warnings against the 81 the P2
  campaign recorded, and the extra one is not a lint — it is
  `File system loop found: test-vectors/bench/bench points to an ancestor`,
  from the symlink this worktree needs for `photo_4k.avif` (the primary
  checkout has the same self-referential link). Zero lints in the touched
  files.
* `forbid(unsafe_code)` holds; no `unsafe` added. clippy clean on the touched
  crate, `--release --all-targets`.

## Reproducing

  # builds are nice'd; timed runs are NOT
  CARGO_TARGET_DIR=~/tmp/rav1d-p3/tgt-head nice -n 19 cargo build --release \
    --example bench_ab_decode -j 4          # + --features shards-32 / shards-64
                                            #   / probe-untracked for the arms
  CELLS="v4k_8tile:1 v4k_8tile:4 v4k_8tile:8" ARMS="head adapt" \
    ./scripts/perf/p3_sweep.sh out.tsv 3 3 3
